1
00:00:01,910 --> 00:00:12,748
Hello everyone.

2
00:00:12,748 --> 00:00:58,270
My name is Huy, and today I will share how to perform manual dSD testing using the Google Test framework.

3
00:00:58,270 --> 00:01:26,310
The initial steps are similar to creating a normal GTest project, but we also add rules to check preprocessor directives and test comments that describe the code.

4
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:41,974
First, create a GTest project, open the source file, and check its preprocessor directives.

5
00:01:41,974 --> 00:01:55,400
Then create corresponding test scripts to enable and disable those directives.

6
00:01:55,400 --> 00:02:28,190
Next, design the test cases, build the module, add descriptions, generate a test specification, demonstrate an archive example, and explain what reviewers should check.

7
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:57,460
To create a project, create a folder named after the module, then drag the C file onto the batch file to generate the GTest project.

8
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:03,890
A console window will appear.

9
00:03:03,890 --> 00:03:15,450
Next, define all variables and macros and initialize any missing data types in the C file.

10
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:24,570
This step was covered in the previous session, so I will not repeat it.

11
00:03:30,180 --> 00:03:35,414
Second, check the preprocessor directives.

12
00:03:35,414 --> 00:03:49,370
Open the .c file and scan the code from beginning to end to see whether it contains any preprocessor directives.

13
00:03:49,370 --> 00:04:01,513
In this example, the function contains a preprocessor directive.

14
00:04:01,513 --> 00:04:17,450
In the header file, we must cover both cases: enabling and disabling that directive.

15
00:04:19,250 --> 00:04:29,980
In the header file, I define ON_TRIGGER as 1 and also create OFF_TRIGGER.

16
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:38,800
Then create configurations to enable or disable these cases.

17
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:47,700
Do you have any questions about this part?

18
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:54,689
Here is another example.

19
00:04:54,689 --> 00:05:22,350
When the source has many preprocessor directives, do not create a separate configuration for every case.

20
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:52,670
If their suffix is _ON, create only two configurations: enable all preprocessor directives or disable all of them.

21
00:05:52,670 --> 00:06:13,498
In another case, one identifier is compared with multiple suffixes.

22
00:06:13,498 --> 00:06:34,948
Create scripts that generate coverage for each different suffix case.

23
00:06:34,948 --> 00:06:45,828
Does everyone understand this part?

24
00:06:45,828 --> 00:06:53,600
Are there any questions?

25
00:06:53,600 --> 00:07:02,240
The third part is test case design.

26
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:43,220
To make dSD mapping easier, number test cases consecutively from 1 through the final test case, regardless of whether a macro is enabled or disabled in a test script.

27
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:56,860
The first test case for each function must be numbered first and should cover as much as possible.

28
00:07:56,860 --> 00:08:10,500
In this example, the first test case covers both branches at line 93 and both branches at line 95.

29
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:24,945
Third, check the effective values using EXPECT_EQ in Google Test.

30
00:08:24,945 --> 00:08:56,723
Check the function return value, global variables, expected values of global static variables, and variables that the function uses or updates.

31
00:08:56,723 --> 00:09:13,390
For a Boolean function, create a return-value variable and check its value.

32
00:09:17,240 --> 00:09:32,790
I will find the remaining examples and update this slide later.

33
00:09:32,790 --> 00:09:44,809
After designing all test cases, build the module.

34
00:09:44,809 --> 00:10:13,753
The build log shows the total number of designed and executed test cases, as well as the achieved coverage percentage.

35
00:10:13,753 --> 00:10:27,489
Why does this log jump from test case 8 to test case 14?

36
00:10:27,489 --> 00:10:37,300
Did I number the test cases incorrectly?

37
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:52,343
Could someone answer this question?

38
00:10:52,343 --> 00:11:19,420
I have not run this part before, so I am not sure how it works.

39
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:36,223
As explained earlier, multiple test scripts are created to cover the function.

40
00:11:36,223 --> 00:12:08,320
Test cases 9 through 13 are disabled by a macro in the .h file, so they are not covered by this test script; they are covered by another test script.

41
00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:18,660
In that script, test case 14 onward is disabled.

42
00:12:18,660 --> 00:12:41,293
Next, add test descriptions to the test cases.

43
00:12:41,293 --> 00:13:10,815
Use the GenTestSpec tool to add comments for each test case.

44
00:13:10,815 --> 00:14:05,430
Use the latest version, select the main stream channel, and provide the path to the project that needs testing.

45
00:14:05,430 --> 00:14:31,190
The comment form shows the test case number, function name, test goal, precondition, and test steps.

46
00:14:31,190 --> 00:14:57,980
When writing a test-case description, all variables and operators must be expressed in natural language.

47
00:14:57,980 --> 00:15:32,680
For example, write comparison operators as “not equal to” or “equal to,” and write A[0] as “array A at index 0.”

48
00:15:32,680 --> 00:15:34,883
The second rule is for MCDC coverage.

49
00:15:34,883 --> 00:15:39,468
Do not write that the test case covers two folds or two folds of a condition.

50
00:15:39,468 --> 00:15:45,660
Instead, write “Focus on below implementation” and list every case behavior that the test case performs.

51
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:57,405
For the positive case, the condition is true when A equals 1 and C is greater than or equal to 2.

52
00:15:57,405 --> 00:16:07,212
For the second case, check the negative case of condition A: A is not equal to 1.

53
00:16:07,212 --> 00:16:15,324
Because this is an AND condition, C does not need to be considered.

54
00:16:15,324 --> 00:16:21,620
For the third case, A equals 1 and C is less than 2.

55
00:16:21,620 --> 00:16:41,586
For the first test case, write: “Check for the positive case of condition A is equal to 1 and C is greater than or equal to 2.”

56
00:16:41,586 --> 00:16:54,320
Then add “Focus on below implementation” and list the values assigned to A and C.

57
00:16:54,320 --> 00:17:10,281
For test case 2, which covers the false case, write: “Check for the negative case of condition A is equal to 1.”

58
00:17:10,281 --> 00:17:15,411
Add “Focus on below implementation.”

59
00:17:15,411 --> 00:17:31,800
Because A does not satisfy the condition, the following condition is unnecessary and does not need to be mentioned.

60
00:17:31,800 --> 00:17:39,255
Test case 3 covers the other false case.

61
00:17:39,255 --> 00:17:57,520
Write it similarly to test case 2, but also describe the value assigned to the remaining variable.

62
00:17:57,520 --> 00:18:08,132
Do you have any questions?

63
00:18:08,132 --> 00:18:46,499
The descriptions of the behavior performed by the function are written in the Test Goal field.

64
00:18:46,499 --> 00:19:42,010
The tool automatically transfers declarations and input values from the test case into the Precondition and Input fields of the comment.

65
00:19:42,010 --> 00:20:11,146
There is also a tool that converts the information from the test file into an Excel test specification file.

66
00:20:11,146 --> 00:20:18,700
I will demonstrate it later.

67
00:20:18,700 --> 00:20:32,256
Next, I will demonstrate a module called CycleHandle.

68
00:20:32,256 --> 00:20:43,510
The slide provides the .c file for practice.

69
00:20:43,510 --> 00:21:02,950
I have prepared the initial steps already, including defining the variables.

70
00:21:02,950 --> 00:21:26,050
The .cpp file should expose the necessary functions from the .c file, initialize static functions, expose global variables, mock methods, and define stub functions.

71
00:21:26,050 --> 00:21:36,122
First, open the .c file and scan it.

72
00:21:36,122 --> 00:21:59,624
It contains a preprocessor directive, so determine how many test scripts are needed.

73
00:21:59,624 --> 00:22:32,920
Here, two scripts are sufficient: one to disable the directive and one to enable it for testing the corresponding code.

74
00:22:32,920 --> 00:22:39,834
Confirm the test script.

75
00:22:39,834 --> 00:23:09,703
The identifier is set to the OFF configuration, or the opposite configuration when the directive is enabled.

76
00:23:09,703 --> 00:23:21,595
Then perform a clean build and build again.

77
00:23:21,595 --> 00:23:39,849
Whenever you modify the .c or .h file, you must clean and rebuild.

78
00:23:39,849 --> 00:23:47,870
This build log has no errors.

79
00:23:47,870 --> 00:24:00,018
Move to the .cpp file and design a test case.

80
00:24:00,018 --> 00:24:12,976
This is the first function, so call it directly.

81
00:24:12,976 --> 00:24:27,014
It has no parameters, so leave the parameters empty.

82
00:24:27,014 --> 00:24:51,310
Delete unused variables, and because it is a void function, remove the return-value check.

83
00:24:51,310 --> 00:25:11,054
As mentioned on the slide, the first test case should cover as much as possible.

84
00:25:11,054 --> 00:25:24,875
I will cover the true case for the condition at line 64.

85
00:25:24,875 --> 00:25:51,283
This line compares the function return value with E_OK; it is true when they are equal and false otherwise.

86
00:25:51,283 --> 00:25:59,180
The function has two parameters.

87
00:25:59,180 --> 00:26:21,263
The mock uses WillRepeatedly to return E_OK, so the first condition is true.

88
00:26:21,263 --> 00:26:34,920
We also need to cover the true case at line 66.

89
00:26:34,920 --> 00:26:53,226
The CycleHandleQualifier is an input variable of this function.

90
00:26:53,226 --> 00:27:10,660
For the second variable, use the Google Test SetBody syntax.

91
00:27:10,660 --> 00:27:59,768
Set the value at the second parameter position to 1, then set the return value so that it is compared with false again.

92
00:27:59,768 --> 00:28:58,780
The warning indicates that execution entered the true case and that an additional item still needs to be added with the correct type and value.

93
00:28:58,780 --> 00:29:57,220
To confirm that the code path has been entered, open the module coverage result in Cantata and check the relevant report.

94
00:29:57,220 --> 00:30:26,175
The coverage view shows what test case 1 has covered.

95
00:30:26,175 --> 00:31:48,670
Add notes that record the behavior covered by the test case, so that when comments are added later, it is clear which behavior each test case performs.

96
00:31:48,670 --> 00:32:02,287
When building, a redefinition error may occur.

97
00:32:02,287 --> 00:32:28,041
This means test case 1 has already been generated and has been created again elsewhere.

98
00:32:28,041 --> 00:32:40,770
Correct the duplicate test case definition.

99
00:32:40,770 --> 00:33:26,801
After fixing the errors, a green check mark indicates that the test is successful.

100
00:33:26,801 --> 00:34:17,884
Open the coverage view and double-click the relevant .cpp file to verify the covered paths.

101
00:34:17,884 --> 00:34:57,740
Create additional test cases as needed to cover the remaining branches.

102
00:34:57,740 --> 00:35:10,582
The current test script covers one preprocessor configuration.

103
00:35:10,582 --> 00:35:31,295
To test the other case, disable the current configuration and enable the alternative in the .h file.

104
00:35:31,295 --> 00:35:46,830
The associated test cases in the .cpp file are then disabled automatically.

105
00:35:46,830 --> 00:36:00,500
This is how multiple test scripts can be created in one .cpp file.

106
00:36:00,500 --> 00:36:11,360
Do you have any questions?

107
00:36:11,360 --> 00:36:27,667
Could you show where the configuration is placed in the .cpp file?

108
00:36:27,667 --> 00:37:05,965
It can be placed at the bottom, but when a function is not inside a configuration, keeping the configuration outside can make the test script more complex.

109
00:37:05,965 --> 00:37:38,580
For each macro that must be covered, use the corresponding conditional block only for its test cases, keeping the test script clean.

110
00:37:38,580 --> 00:38:16,165
After modifying the .h file, clean and build the project again.

111
00:38:16,165 --> 00:39:11,050
The tool may lag, but when the OFF configuration is selected, the lower section is disabled.

112
00:39:11,050 --> 00:39:37,716
Rename the test case so it is clear which function it covers.

113
00:39:37,716 --> 00:40:10,065
Although any name is possible, it is recommended to use the function name.

114
00:40:10,065 --> 00:40:45,474
There is an error here: test case numbers must start at 1 and remain consecutive.

115
00:40:45,474 --> 00:41:12,140
The test case number 3 was used again, so it must be changed.

116
00:41:12,140 --> 00:41:27,915
Should test case names be numbered by function or consecutively from top to bottom?

117
00:41:27,915 --> 00:41:53,193
For example, if there are ten functions and each function needs four or five test cases, should numbering continue as 15, 16, and 17?

118
00:41:53,193 --> 00:42:08,018
Yes, according to the guideline, the next test case is numbered consecutively.

119
00:42:08,018 --> 00:42:16,000
The purpose is to make dSD mapping easier.

120
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:35,912
The numbering is not intended to show how many test cases belong to a function.

121
00:42:35,912 --> 00:43:02,126
The rule is to number every test case consecutively from the first to the final test case in the C file.

122
00:43:02,126 --> 00:43:36,910
For example, if the first function needs five test cases, they are numbered 1 through 5; the next function continues from the next number.

123
00:43:36,910 --> 00:43:55,236
For example, suppose the Trigger function originally uses test cases 1 through 5, and SignalValid uses test case 6.

124
00:43:55,236 --> 00:44:13,403
If a requirement later changes SetTrigger and an additional test case is needed, that new test case is numbered 7.

125
00:44:13,403 --> 00:44:29,180
Although it concerns Trigger, its number remains 7 because numbering is continuous across the file.

126
00:44:29,180 --> 00:44:46,252
The participant notes that the naming rule can be confusing.

127
00:44:46,252 --> 00:45:34,340
For example, if a function originally has six test cases and a new test case is added with number 7, a reviewer might mistakenly think the function has seven test cases.

128
00:45:34,340 --> 00:45:54,103
The response is that a test case name has two parts: the function name covered by the test case and the sequence number.

129
00:45:54,103 --> 00:46:04,149
When adding a new test case, assign the next sequence number.

130
00:46:04,149 --> 00:46:14,690
The sequence number is not limited by the function being tested.

131
00:46:14,690 --> 00:46:35,848
In the build output, the function-name part identifies which function test case 1 covers.

132
00:46:35,848 --> 00:46:56,530
The trailing number is simply a normal consecutive number, such as 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, or 7.

133
00:46:56,530 --> 00:47:34,509
For example, if a test script already has test cases 1 through 10 to cover the implementation in the current version, and a new requirement is added for the Deactivated function, you may add a new test case named Deactivated_TC11.

134
00:47:34,509 --> 00:47:41,610
The mapping section will make this clearer.

135
00:47:41,610 --> 00:48:03,116
The participant is concerned that a report could show Deactivated test cases 1, 2, 3, and 5, followed by SignalValid test case 4.

136
00:48:03,116 --> 00:48:13,785
This might make a reviewer ask where Deactivated test case 4 is.

137
00:48:13,785 --> 00:48:32,790
They suggest numbering test cases separately within each function to make the quantity per function easier to see.

138
00:48:32,790 --> 00:48:51,284
The build runs test cases from top to bottom; it does not group them by the function-name portion of the test case name.

139
00:48:51,284 --> 00:49:01,610
That name is only displayed and does not determine the build order.

140
00:49:01,610 --> 00:49:24,594
When a change point requires a new test case, it can be added below the related test case to make tracking easier.

141
00:49:24,594 --> 00:49:41,530
Otherwise, inserting it in the middle requires renumbering all following test cases.

142
00:49:41,530 --> 00:50:00,729
Does naming test cases by function and by the number of test cases in that function affect later processes?

143
00:50:00,729 --> 00:50:05,215
No.

144
00:50:05,215 --> 00:50:31,590
However, grouping related changes in one place may make later delta work more difficult because subsequent additions can still require renumbering.

145
00:50:31,590 --> 00:50:55,233
The participant suggests restarting numbering for each function, such as SignalValid_TC1 and Deactivated_TC1 through TC3.

146
00:50:55,233 --> 00:51:09,106
The response is that mapping must then carry the full naming structure.

147
00:51:09,106 --> 00:51:41,150
The current convention is intended to make mapping easier, but it is a general guideline rather than an absolute rule; teams may adapt it to their project workflow.

148
00:51:41,150 --> 00:51:51,387
Another participant argues that the process should be standardized across teams to avoid complications when projects are transferred.

149
00:51:51,387 --> 00:51:59,700
The open issue is how mapping handles the naming convention, so it will be revisited in the mapping section.

150
00:51:59,700 --> 00:52:15,624
What is the difference between TEST_F and TEST?

151
00:52:15,624 --> 00:52:35,953
TEST_F is a Google Test syntax used in the generated format.

152
00:52:35,953 --> 00:52:58,993
Earlier work often used TEST, which has a slightly different format.

153
00:52:58,993 --> 00:53:26,437
In this case, all test cases are grouped in a Unit Test class, so TEST_F is used.

154
00:53:26,437 --> 00:53:45,750
The speaker is not certain of every technical difference.

155
00:53:45,750 --> 00:53:59,293
Could the mapping be shown first?

156
00:53:59,293 --> 00:54:53,056
A participant again recommends starting test-case numbering at 1 for each function because it may be easier to maintain and review.

157
00:54:53,056 --> 00:55:57,080
The response is that teams can choose a convention, but the mapping tool parses the test case name into the function name and the test case sequence number.

158
00:55:57,080 --> 00:56:26,096
To make this clear, the presenter will show the mapping.

159
00:56:26,096 --> 00:57:09,620
Huy has generated a test specification, and it can be used as the basis for mapping.

160
00:57:09,620 --> 00:57:19,495
You can map from the generated Test Spec or from the test script that has just been covered.

161
00:57:19,495 --> 00:57:35,060
This example Test Spec was generated with Cantata because GTest is not installed on this machine, but the mapping method is essentially the same.

162
00:57:35,060 --> 00:58:15,498
In the Test Spec, the relevant column describes the flow or behavior executed by each test case.

163
00:58:15,498 --> 00:58:36,980
The comments follow the test-case flow in the code.

164
00:58:36,980 --> 00:58:54,516
The mapping interface is Enterprise Architect, also called dSD.

165
00:58:54,516 --> 00:59:07,320
Access to the dSD project depends on the team.

166
00:59:07,320 --> 00:59:44,340
In the main stream, use the CSWA application and contact the person who manages the EA repository or the PCM source to obtain access.

167
00:59:44,340 --> 01:00:02,871
To find the file to map, search for the module name, for example SignalMonitoringLine, using Ctrl+F.

168
01:00:02,871 --> 01:00:32,520
The search may limit the number of characters, so for long names, search using the distinctive suffix because the beginning of module names is often duplicated.

169
01:00:32,520 --> 01:00:50,680
From the results, find the object with the package or folder icon and use Find in Project Browser.

170
01:00:50,680 --> 01:01:03,747
The dSD database may contain several copies of the same module, such as SignalMonitoringLine.

171
01:01:03,747 --> 01:01:16,533
This can occur when a project copies content from a platform without removing the original.

172
01:01:16,533 --> 01:01:35,360
Carefully check that the package selected is the one currently under test, rather than an old platform copy that has not been updated.

173
01:01:35,360 --> 01:01:49,454
Pay attention to the colored exclamation mark.

174
01:01:49,454 --> 01:02:07,531
A package without a user lock can be viewed but not edited.

175
01:02:07,531 --> 01:02:36,945
A green exclamation mark means the package and its children are locked by you and can be edited.

176
01:02:36,945 --> 01:03:07,890
Use Apply User Lock to lock the package; after it is locked, the green exclamation mark is displayed.

177
01:03:07,890 --> 01:03:18,223
On your machine, a green exclamation mark means you locked the module.

178
01:03:18,223 --> 01:03:24,275
If it is red, another user has locked it.

179
01:03:24,275 --> 01:03:41,400
To edit the module, contact that user and ask them to release the lock; then apply the lock yourself before editing.

180
01:03:41,400 --> 01:03:47,533
This is a diagram.

181
01:03:47,533 --> 01:04:06,914
First, make sure that everything in the code is represented in the dSD diagram.

182
01:04:06,914 --> 01:04:35,618
Developers should include variable declarations, assigned values, true and false conditions, actions, and any macros.

183
01:04:35,618 --> 01:05:02,360
Even when the current example has no macro, if the source code has one, it must also be drawn in the diagram.

184
01:05:02,360 --> 01:05:38,335
Teams may depict a macro as a true/false condition using a diamond, often in a different color such as orange or green.

185
01:05:38,335 --> 01:05:50,125
The macro condition must be documented.

186
01:05:50,125 --> 01:06:28,820
Usually place it in the node heading so that readers can see it immediately; use the lower field only when the code is too long.

187
01:06:28,820 --> 01:06:42,464
When the code is long, place the code in the lower field and use the node heading to describe the function of the node.

188
01:06:42,464 --> 01:06:45,560
This makes auditing easier.

189
01:06:45,560 --> 01:06:52,439
Auditors may ask how the Test Spec was derived from the dSD.

190
01:06:52,439 --> 01:07:05,969
If the dSD lacks complete condition, statement, or decision information, the team cannot answer that question clearly.

191
01:07:05,969 --> 01:07:13,078
Ensure the diagram accurately and fully reflects the function.

192
01:07:13,078 --> 01:07:22,480
Arrays defined outside the function can be documented outside the overall diagram.

193
01:07:22,480 --> 01:07:27,483
Now for mapping.

194
01:07:27,483 --> 01:07:44,094
The test-case numbering convention is used to make mapping into the diagram easier.

195
01:07:44,094 --> 01:07:57,904
Declarations that simply flow straight down do not need to be listed.

196
01:07:57,904 --> 01:08:20,320
When test case 1 reaches the first condition and takes the true path, attach the TC1 mapping label to that path.

197
01:08:23,080 --> 01:08:31,577
Cantata supports separate test scripts for different configurations.

198
01:08:31,577 --> 01:08:45,448
GTest can also use two scripts, but the second script requires substantial configuration and is time-consuming.

199
01:08:45,448 --> 01:08:58,819
Therefore, use one script and distinct test-case numbers so that each test case can be found by its number.

200
01:08:58,819 --> 01:09:10,440
The concern about numbering is reasonable, but the convention supports this mapping workflow.

201
01:09:10,440 --> 01:09:21,320
You could use a different number sequence in another part of the diagram, such as 1, 2, 4, and 5.

202
01:09:21,320 --> 01:09:38,480
However, this makes searching and identifying the correct mapping more laborious because several diagram areas may contain similarly numbered test cases.

203
01:09:38,480 --> 01:09:54,435
To create a mapping label, open the required element and add a label such as TS1_TC12, then click OK.

204
01:09:54,435 --> 01:10:00,754
Place it on the corresponding true path.

205
01:10:00,754 --> 01:10:09,443
Also label the connecting path itself as true or false.

206
01:10:09,443 --> 01:10:26,030
Developers sometimes draw a path without specifying whether it is true or false, which must be corrected.

207
01:10:26,030 --> 01:10:43,884
When a path direction is unclear, label it directly as false, or set its stereotype to Yes or No.

208
01:10:43,884 --> 01:10:54,560
This displays the corresponding path label in the diagram.

209
01:10:54,560 --> 01:11:12,569
After following the true path, use the Test Spec to determine what the next condition covers, then attach the same TC1 label to the appropriate path.

210
01:11:12,569 --> 01:11:21,030
To copy a label, hold Ctrl and drag it, then paste the copied element.

211
01:11:21,030 --> 01:11:48,247
If you know how many occurrences of a test-case label are needed, create several labels at once.

212
01:11:48,247 --> 01:12:23,686
Open the User Interface, hold Ctrl, right-click, select Create Multiple Elements, choose Label, and enter the test case name.

213
01:12:23,686 --> 01:12:42,114
Add the required number of rows, for example five, then click OK.

214
01:12:42,114 --> 01:12:57,140
Drag each generated label to the correct mapped path.

215
01:12:57,140 --> 01:13:25,069
Continue mapping test case 1 until its final path, then map test cases 2, 3, 4, and so on.

216
01:13:25,069 --> 01:13:42,137
Follow the behavior described in the Test Spec comment.

217
01:13:42,137 --> 01:14:24,030
Map only the paths that the comment says the test case covers; do not map unrelated paths merely because execution passed through them.

218
01:14:24,030 --> 01:14:29,326
Map MCDC test cases as well.

219
01:14:29,326 --> 01:14:46,537
When a later version adds test cases, such as TC99 and TC100, map the added MCDC cases too.

220
01:14:46,537 --> 01:15:05,640
Otherwise, an auditor may ask why a test case was created but was not mapped in Enterprise Architect.

221
01:15:06,640 --> 01:15:25,556
A participant asks how this works for Docizen, where the model is created from code syntax.

222
01:15:25,556 --> 01:15:48,213
The presenter has not used that approach with SWEDM; current projects primarily use the main-stream workflow.

223
01:15:48,213 --> 01:16:13,780
Treat this as a basic process for qualifying the workflow, then discuss with the team how to adapt it to the specific tool.

224
01:16:13,780 --> 01:16:46,580
The main benefits of mapping are verifying that the covered flow is correct and confirming that the information in the dSD is complete and accurate.

225
01:16:46,580 --> 01:17:04,267
After finishing the mapping, remember to release the user lock so that other people can edit the module.

226
01:17:04,267 --> 01:17:10,560
Otherwise, the module remains locked.

227
01:17:10,560 --> 01:17:19,117
To release the lock, right-click the package, choose Release User Lock, and click OK.

228
01:17:19,117 --> 01:17:22,640
The lock is released automatically.

229
01:17:22,640 --> 01:17:47,425
Some people export the file to work locally for better performance and then import it back into Enterprise Architect.

230
01:17:47,425 --> 01:18:11,998
This is possible, but it has a serious limitation: links to headers or interface elements can be lost during export.

231
01:18:11,998 --> 01:18:28,945
Importing the local package back into the project can remove those header links.

232
01:18:28,945 --> 01:18:46,740
Work carefully; despite slower performance, editing directly in EA avoids this risk.

233
01:18:46,740 --> 01:18:49,335
Please continue, Huy.

234
01:18:49,335 --> 01:18:51,930
Thank you.

235
01:18:51,930 --> 01:19:07,607
We have finished the test-case numbering and covered this function.

236
01:19:07,607 --> 01:19:28,900
The next step is not to view the Test Spec but to add the comment format to the test cases.

237
01:19:28,900 --> 01:19:58,999
Open the tool, select the main stream channel, and choose only the outer project folder path.

238
01:19:58,999 --> 01:20:18,094
Do not select the test .cpp file or other internal folders.

239
01:20:18,094 --> 01:21:08,260
The tool automatically generates the comment format in the .cpp file, but verify that every generated test-case number matches the corresponding test case.

240
01:21:08,260 --> 01:21:36,903
To write the comment, first build an outline of the test-case flow.

241
01:21:36,903 --> 01:22:43,594
Describe every relevant operation shown in the test case: declarations, assigned values, conditions, the paths that are covered, and the subsequent actions.

242
01:22:43,594 --> 01:23:01,550
Leave spacing so the flow is easy to read.

243
01:23:01,550 --> 01:23:41,477
Convert all symbols and operators into natural language.

244
01:23:41,477 --> 01:25:32,702
For the condition comparing the return value of CycleQualifier with E_OK, write: “Check the true case: the return value of CycleQualifier is equal to E_OK.”

245
01:25:32,702 --> 01:26:03,360
State that the return value is set to E_OK.

246
01:26:03,360 --> 01:26:43,275
Write the same type of description for the following condition.

247
01:26:43,275 --> 01:27:30,793
State the true case, the relevant return value, and the value it is set to.

248
01:27:30,793 --> 01:28:15,777
Then describe that the true condition calls the corresponding function.

249
01:28:15,777 --> 01:29:02,661
This completes the description for the true cases at conditions 64 and 66.

250
01:29:02,661 --> 01:29:18,500
Delete any unused text.

251
01:29:18,500 --> 01:29:27,068
Do you have any questions?

252
01:29:27,068 --> 01:30:06,610
Once the generated comment format is in place, delete the temporary notes that were added while designing the test case.

253
01:30:06,610 --> 01:30:58,647
For test case 2, do not repeat initialization steps that were already described in test case 1 and do not affect the condition.

254
01:30:58,647 --> 01:31:20,773
Describe only what this test case covers and performs.

255
01:31:20,773 --> 01:31:44,948
Here, it checks the false case of the condition at line 66.

256
01:31:44,948 --> 01:32:28,380
Remove any redundant content generated by the tool, and describe the next false condition in the same way.

257
01:32:28,380 --> 01:33:06,200
For test case 3, check the false case of the outer condition.

258
01:33:06,200 --> 01:34:12,540
Copy the structure of the description, then verify what values are actually set, such as E_OK and not E_OK.

259
01:34:12,540 --> 01:35:30,040
After completing the comments for the first function, review them and convert every remaining operator into natural language.

260
01:35:30,040 --> 01:36:43,278
To work faster in VS Code, select a repeated token and press Ctrl+D to find the next matching occurrence and edit it.

261
01:36:43,278 --> 01:38:25,310
A participant notes that Ctrl+D appears to select a whole line in their environment; no custom shortcut settings were used, so they will investigate it separately.

262
01:38:25,310 --> 01:38:39,361
Do you have any questions about comments?

263
01:38:39,361 --> 01:39:33,510
For the next function, remember that the tool transfers declarations into the Precondition section and transfers Set Value information into the Input section.

264
01:39:33,510 --> 01:40:57,191
Please write the first test-case description for this function.

265
01:40:57,191 --> 01:42:16,888
Post a Test Goal for test cases 4 and 5 in the meeting chat.

266
01:42:16,888 --> 01:43:24,630
This test case follows the true branch to line 127.

267
01:43:24,630 --> 01:43:58,422
For this example, declare the required old-value and new-value variables, then check the true case.

268
01:43:58,422 --> 01:44:30,166
Note that the called function is an actual function, not a stubbed variable in the .cpp file.

269
01:44:30,166 --> 01:45:24,438
It returns true or false based on its return value; when CycleInfo.qualifier is valid, it returns 1, and when it is invalid or another value, it returns false.

270
01:45:24,438 --> 01:46:08,470
Therefore, writing “the return value of this function is true” is sufficient; the exact numeric value does not need to be stated.

271
01:46:08,470 --> 01:46:23,142
After checking the true case, cover the MCDC condition.

272
01:46:23,142 --> 01:47:15,160
Because the expression contains an AND operator, add “Focus on below implementation,” state that the relevant value is set to true and the old value is set to false, then set the trigger to true.

273
01:47:15,160 --> 01:47:53,928
When the trigger is set to true, execution enters the condition, reaches line 134, assigns the new value to the old value, and returns the result.

274
01:47:53,928 --> 01:48:15,170
Because this function returns a Boolean value, its return value must be checked.

275
01:48:17,150 --> 01:49:02,701
The build shows that the EXPECT_EQ check expects 0, but the actual return value is 1, so the check fails.

276
01:49:02,701 --> 01:49:26,561
Modify the expected value to 1 so that the test passes.

277
01:49:26,561 --> 01:49:49,120
Apply the same correction to the related test cases.

278
01:49:49,120 --> 01:50:20,425
For the next test case, do not repeat previous descriptions.

279
01:50:20,425 --> 01:51:20,425
Cover the required MCDC false case and use “Focus on below implementation” to state the relevant new-value setting.

280
01:51:20,425 --> 01:52:30,860
This test case is focused on this specific condition only, so do not repeat comments that were already included in the first test case.

281
01:52:49,860 --> 01:53:33,162
The next test case covers the remaining true/false case for the condition at line 121.

282
01:53:33,162 --> 01:54:36,100
Use a focused description that states the necessary input values and the expected false result of the condition and function.

283
01:54:36,100 --> 01:54:42,325
Why is this better?

284
01:54:42,325 --> 01:55:04,985
This is a real function, so its behavior is determined by its actual code and input values.

285
01:55:04,985 --> 01:55:33,871
In GTest, real functions cannot be stubbed; you must inspect the code to see what return value drives the condition.

286
01:55:33,871 --> 01:55:59,520
Cantata can use a replay wrapper to simulate a real function, but GTest does not support that approach.

287
01:55:59,520 --> 01:56:18,411
Now move to the second test script.

288
01:56:18,411 --> 01:57:16,704
The test cases covered by test script 1 are disabled, and the next script continues with its own test cases.

289
01:57:16,704 --> 01:58:05,282
Values still need to be initialized correctly; otherwise the build log will show an error.

290
01:58:05,282 --> 01:59:15,450
Even when a function is not related to the earlier test script, it must be covered again from the beginning in this configuration.

291
01:59:15,450 --> 01:59:45,305
There is a gap when adding comments across scripts.

292
01:59:45,305 --> 02:00:25,698
Re-running the comment tool can generate overlapping comment formats.

293
02:00:25,698 --> 02:01:41,800
The tool places a generic final test-case comment on the last test case, but it contains no useful information and must be edited.

294
02:01:46,840 --> 02:02:21,900
It is recommended to design and cover every test case in every test script first, then generate and write the comments.

295
02:02:21,900 --> 02:02:48,710
The first test case of test script 2 must describe all behaviors it covers in the function.

296
02:02:48,710 --> 02:03:35,690
For faster editing, place the cursor at the final position and drag down to create multiple cursors, then edit the repeated content together.

297
02:03:35,690 --> 02:04:36,699
Use the multi-cursor editing shortcut to copy or modify repeated text more quickly.

298
02:04:36,699 --> 02:05:31,092
Then verify the next condition and complete its corresponding description.

299
02:05:31,092 --> 02:06:18,870
You can copy the completed description above as a starting point.

300
02:07:00,820 --> 02:07:17,562
Continue with test cases 10 and 11.

301
02:07:17,562 --> 02:07:52,480
The build log should show coverage from test case 4 through test case 11.

302
02:07:52,480 --> 02:08:32,660
Because the preprocessor configuration is enabled, the remaining cases are disabled.

303
02:08:32,660 --> 02:08:57,533
Do you have any questions about the comment section?

304
02:08:57,533 --> 02:09:12,840
We will take a ten-minute break.

305
02:09:12,840 --> 02:20:01,430
After the break, the next section covers how to generate a Test Spec.

306
02:20:01,430 --> 02:20:22,068
Open the latest main-stream version of the tool.

307
02:20:22,068 --> 02:21:08,934
I recommend copying each test script into a separate temporary file, such as Test Script 1 and Test Script 2.

308
02:21:08,934 --> 02:21:48,490
Copy common functions that are covered by both scripts into the appropriate temporary files.

309
02:21:48,490 --> 02:22:45,961
Provide the folder path, select the test sequence, and set the Test Name.

310
02:22:45,961 --> 02:24:03,900
For Test Script 1, use a clear name such as Config1, then provide the corresponding test .cpp file.

311
02:24:03,900 --> 02:25:15,399
In the Description field, describe the macro configuration as clearly as possible.

312
02:25:15,399 --> 02:27:13,110
State whether the identifier equals the relevant trigger value and why that is true or false in the selected test script configuration.

313
02:27:13,110 --> 02:27:34,395
Click Run and wait for the Test Spec to be generated.

314
02:27:34,395 --> 02:27:58,492
Ctrl+Click a hyperlink to jump to the function in its sheet.

315
02:27:58,492 --> 02:28:31,022
Update any Cantata references to GTest and verify the generated result carefully.

316
02:28:31,022 --> 02:29:20,420
Deleting a required field in the comment format, such as Precondition, can cause the Test Spec to be generated incorrectly.

317
02:29:20,420 --> 02:30:25,817
Check each generated test case, for example test case 10, and confirm that its Test Goal contains the intended description.

318
02:30:25,817 --> 02:31:31,214
Save the generated file in the delivery location using the module name and the correct version; the default version is 1.0.

319
02:31:31,214 --> 02:32:07,900
Also update author and reviewer information copied from the template.

320
02:32:07,900 --> 02:33:02,080
For test cases that cover MCDC, the Test Description section of the Test Spec includes a “Modified Condition Decision Coverage” line.

321
02:33:02,080 --> 02:33:22,449
This line does not appear for ordinary test cases.

322
02:33:22,449 --> 02:33:42,410
Change all remaining Cantata references to GTest.

323
02:33:42,410 --> 02:34:33,063
After you have a Test Spec and have checked the tests, create an archive using the same tool.

324
02:34:33,063 --> 02:35:41,690
Select the GTest sheet, enter the module name, project path, and reviewer information, then click Archive for the main stream.

325
02:35:41,690 --> 02:36:15,153
Open the generated archive folder, which is named with the date and the main-stream suffix.

326
02:36:15,153 --> 02:36:45,306
Verify that the expected test cases and required files are present and error-free.

327
02:36:45,306 --> 02:37:36,420
Update the archive support file with the Test Script and Test Spec paths, using the project folder path rather than unrelated parent paths.

328
02:37:36,420 --> 02:38:08,011
For the archive, verify the checklist and confirm that the author, reviewer, module name, pipeline, and date are correct.

329
02:38:08,011 --> 02:38:16,888
Add any required supporting files.

330
02:38:16,888 --> 02:38:37,775
If coverage is below 100%, include the required reason or deviation information.

331
02:38:37,775 --> 02:38:54,484
Save, close, zip the archive, and remove any redundant ZIP file.

332
02:38:54,484 --> 02:39:15,370
For Test Design, copy the Test Spec path into the Channel Engine Utility folder.

333
02:39:17,960 --> 02:39:27,448
Now for review.

334
02:39:27,448 --> 02:40:05,399
A reviewer opens the delivered archive and verifies that the project path matches the expected path.

335
02:40:05,399 --> 02:40:20,200
Test Design uses several review layers.

336
02:40:20,200 --> 02:40:39,935
The first review checks the test cases and comments.

337
02:40:39,935 --> 02:41:46,350
Although a simple module is usually accurate, complex modules require a careful and conscientious reviewer because the author may not fully control every code path or comment.

338
02:41:46,350 --> 02:41:57,264
There are two main review approaches.

339
02:41:57,264 --> 02:42:39,740
First, read the code and the comments, verify which preprocessor configuration is enabled, and confirm what each test case is intended to cover.

340
02:42:40,680 --> 02:43:31,229
Then review by debugging: set a breakpoint, clean and rebuild the project, then run Debug or press F5.

341
02:43:31,229 --> 02:44:45,070
If the archive path contains unsupported special characters, extract the archive into a suitable folder first; otherwise GTest may not run correctly.

342
02:44:45,070 --> 02:45:07,892
Read the comment again, then use Step Into to enter the C file and Step Over to execute line by line.

343
02:45:07,892 --> 02:45:30,262
If the condition is correct, execution enters the expected line; if it is false, it skips the line.

344
02:45:30,262 --> 02:45:58,055
Confirm that test case 1 and its comment are correct, then set the next breakpoint and use Continue to move to test case 2.

345
02:45:58,055 --> 02:46:06,640
Verify its false case in the same way.

346
02:46:06,640 --> 02:47:11,302
A second, more time-consuming method is to comment out other test cases, build the project, and inspect the coverage result in Cantata for each remaining test case.

347
02:47:11,302 --> 02:47:31,410
Repeat this for the relevant cases of the function.

348
02:47:31,410 --> 02:48:28,004
A third method is to add print statements in the .c file at the conditions being reviewed, for example “condition 83” and “condition 86.”

349
02:48:28,004 --> 02:48:47,006
After modifying the C file, clean and rebuild.

350
02:48:47,006 --> 02:49:33,685
Read the build log for a specific test case to confirm that it reported the expected true cases and line numbers.

351
02:49:33,685 --> 02:50:00,123
This verifies whether execution entered the intended conditions.

352
02:50:00,123 --> 02:50:17,060
In practice, debugging is usually faster.

353
02:50:26,180 --> 02:50:53,613
For highly complex modules, accurate review can take substantial time.

354
02:50:53,613 --> 02:52:07,683
Real functions, local static variables, and variables whose values are updated inside conditions may make simple mock syntax unsuitable because later calls do not retain the required state.

355
02:52:07,683 --> 02:52:21,400
Trace the actual code flow instead.

356
02:52:21,400 --> 02:52:39,820
Such tests are normally assigned higher effort.

357
02:52:39,820 --> 02:52:55,033
That concludes the presentation.

358
02:52:55,033 --> 02:53:15,000
Are there any questions or unclear points?

359
02:53:15,000 --> 02:53:58,262
You can ask during this session, or contact me later when you encounter an issue in a task.

360
02:53:58,262 --> 02:54:13,000
I will support you where I can.

361
02:54:13,000 --> 02:54:25,203
Could you ZIP the test file shown earlier so we can use it as a reference?

362
02:54:25,203 --> 02:54:30,975
Yes, I will share it in the slides.

363
02:54:30,975 --> 02:54:41,364
If there are no further questions, we will end today’s meeting.

364
02:54:41,364 --> 02:54:45,487
Thank you for joining.

365
02:54:45,487 --> 02:54:49,610
Bye, everyone.
